  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe latest water vapor imagery (~3 AM ET) depicts a potent \nshortwave across southern Wisconsin/Lake Michigan early this \nmorning, with scattered showers and storms beginning to develop \nacross northern Illinois. This area of showers and storms will \nbe the main area to watch this morning and early afternoon as \nthe shortwave continues to quickly move east through the Lower \nGreat Lakes, providing plenty in the way of environmental shear \nto support organized convection. There remains some uncertainty \non the amount of destabilization ahead of the line, mainly west \nof the I-71 corridor, where probabilities of >1000 J/kg of CAPE \nare lower. Further east, along and east of the I-71 corridor, \nsevere weather chances appear to be increasing with a longer \nperiod of daytime heating ahead of the line. Given the \nanticipated line storm mode, damaging wind gusts up to 70 mph \nare the primary concern with a most likely time window between \n11 AM and 2 PM, particularly along and east of the I-71\ncorridor.\n\nAttention then turns to later this afternoon and evening across\nportions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania where a\nsecond area of pre-frontal showers and thunderstorms may \ndevelop. Concern is increasing for a few rotating storms with \nthis round as the low-level hodograph profile becomes more \nfavorable, potentially resulting in a localized tornado risk, \nevident by the slight westward expansion of the 5% tornado \nprobabilities in the latest SPC SWODY1 outlook. These storms\nappear to be driven by mesoscale processes mainly below 700 mb,\nevident by an abundance of dry air aloft, and thus, anticipating\nthis second round of convection to be fairly shallow, similar \nto the type of storms seen across Wisconsin yesterday (7/20).\nConfidence on this second round remains on the lower side,\nespecially with regards to intensity and coverage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA strong cold front will move across the area tonight, ushering\nin a chilly air mass into the region, characterized by 850 mb\ntemperatures falling as low as 6C on Wednesday. Lake-enhanced\nrain showers will be possible throughout Wednesday across \nportions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania in \naddition to ample cloud cover, especially Wednesday morning and \nearly afternoon. As a result, high temperatures may struggle to \nget out of the 60s in some spots. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nA large upper-level ridge across the Western and Central CONUS\nwill gradually expand further east into the Great Lakes by\nFriday into the weekend, resulting in temperatures to return\nnear or slightly above normal in the 80s. However, a series of\nupper-level troughs across Hudson Bay appear poised to block the\neastward progression of the ridge. This pattern would appear to\nfavor increasing shower and thunderstorm chances across the area\nby early next week if trends continue.",
